### lib.fileset: file set functions

The [`lib.fileset`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#sec-functions-library-fileset "lib.fileset: file set functions") library allows you to work with *file sets*.
A file set is a (mathematical) set of local files that can be added to the Nix store for use in Nix derivations.
File sets are easy and safe to use, providing obvious and composable semantics with good error messages to prevent mistakes.

#### Overview

Basics:

- [Implicit coercion from paths to file sets](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#sec-fileset-path-coercion "Implicit coercion from paths to file sets")

- [`lib.fileset.maybeMissing`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.maybeMissing "lib.fileset.maybeMissing"):

    Create a file set from a path that may be missing.

- [`lib.fileset.trace`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.trace "lib.fileset.trace")/[`lib.fileset.traceVal`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.trace "lib.fileset.trace"):

    Pretty-print file sets for debugging.

- [`lib.fileset.toSource`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.toSource "lib.fileset.toSource"):

    Add files in file sets to the store to use as derivation sources.

- [`lib.fileset.toList`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.toList "lib.fileset.toList"):

    The list of files contained in a file set.

Combinators:

- [`lib.fileset.union`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.union "lib.fileset.union")/[`lib.fileset.unions`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.unions "lib.fileset.unions"):

    Create a larger file set from all the files in multiple file sets.

- [`lib.fileset.intersection`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.intersection "lib.fileset.intersection"):

    Create a smaller file set from only the files in both file sets.

- [`lib.fileset.difference`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.difference "lib.fileset.difference"):

    Create a smaller file set containing all files that are in one file set, but not another one.

Filtering:

- [`lib.fileset.fileFilter`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.fileFilter "lib.fileset.fileFilter"):

    Create a file set from all files that satisisfy a predicate in a directory.

Utilities:

- [`lib.fileset.fromSource`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.fromSource "lib.fileset.fromSource"):

    Create a file set from a `lib.sources`-based value.

- [`lib.fileset.gitTracked`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.gitTracked "lib.fileset.gitTracked")/[`lib.fileset.gitTrackedWith`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.gitTrackedWith "lib.fileset.gitTrackedWith"):

    Create a file set from all tracked files in a local Git repository.

If you need more file set functions,
see [this issue](https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/issues/266356) to request it.

#### Implicit coercion from paths to file sets

All functions accepting file sets as arguments can also accept [paths](https://nixos.org/manual/nix/stable/language/values.html#type-path) as arguments.
Such path arguments are implicitly coerced to file sets containing all files under that path:

- A path to a file turns into a file set containing that single file.

- A path to a directory turns into a file set containing all files *recursively* in that directory.

If the path points to a non-existent location, an error is thrown.

### Note

Just like in Git, file sets cannot represent empty directories.
Because of this, a path to a directory that contains no files (recursively) will turn into a file set containing no files.

### Note

File set coercion does *not* add any of the files under the coerced paths to the store.
Only the [`toSource`](/manual/nixpkgs/unstable/#function-library-lib.fileset.toSource "lib.fileset.toSource") function adds files to the Nix store, and only those files contained in the `fileset` argument.
This is in contrast to using [paths in string interpolation](https://nixos.org/manual/nix/stable/language/values.html#type-path), which does add the entire referenced path to the store.

##### Example

Assume we are in a local directory with a file hierarchy like this:

    ├─ a/
    │  ├─ x (file)
    │  └─ b/
    │     └─ y (file)
    └─ c/
       └─ d/

Here’s a listing of which files get included when different path expressions get coerced to file sets:

- `./.` as a file set contains both `a/x` and `a/b/y` (`c/` does not contain any files and is therefore omitted).

- `./a` as a file set contains both `a/x` and `a/b/y`.

- `./a/x` as a file set contains only `a/x`.

- `./a/b` as a file set contains only `a/b/y`.

- `./c` as a file set is empty, since neither `c` nor `c/d` contain any files.

